# General Rules

- Assume I am on a UNIX-like OS
- Concise and succinct answers are my preferred response
- Tasks are defined in ~/.claude/tasks/*.md / *.org files. These are long running TODO items.

# Writing Code

- Test first approach is required. Write tests first, then implement against them.
- Agentic flow is required. If no agents exist, spin them up.
- Agents should spin up other agents to do implementation or code reviews

# Ownership

- You have ownership of anything in the local environment
- You have no write access in production.
- If the user asks for queries or code to run in production, that is fine. The user will own execution.

# Git

- Prefer using git worktrees for isolated work, especially when multiple Claudes are working in parallel.
- Create worktrees under `~/.claude/worktrees/`, NOT under the project's `.claude/` directory. Keep the project's `.claude` dir clean.
- Branch names must be prefixed with `bryan/` (e.g. `bryan/enterprise-entitlements`).
- Example: `git worktree add ~/.claude/worktrees/<name> -b bryan/<name> <base-branch>`
- Never commit. The user likes to own comitting

# Project Management

- Files represent categories of projects i.e. work.md / work.org -> work projects
- Specific projects are defined in each file

# Planning

- The user is a heavy claude code user and manages projects and tasks from ~/.claude
- Repos that leverage claude will have a .claude folder. If it doesn't exist, assume the user doesn't want claude in there

## Problem Statement and Solution

- Projects have a problem statement. This cannot be edited by anyone but the user.
- Projects have a proposed solution. This cannot be edited by anyone but the user.

### Planning Work
- Once the user approves a plan, tasks should be broken down under the project in the .md or .org file
- Link the claude plan.md file to the project in the .md or .org file
- Ask the user to approve the work breakdown before moving forward. Apply any feedback.

#### Tasks v Subtasks

- Tasks and subtasks always require an agent team
- Tasks and subtasks can be modified by anyone
- Tasks and subtasks should always be done in plan mode
- Solution can only be deemed "shipped" by the user but tasks and subtasks can be marked as complete by the agent
- Tasks and subtasks can be worked on in parallel since we leverage worktrees
- Small, focused tasks are better than a task that says "implement feature"

### Project Priorities

- Projects use a PN priority system
  - P0: Critical
  - P1: High
  - P2: Important
  - P3: Low
  - P4: Nice to have
- P0 can be implemented at any time
- P1 can be implemented at any time after P0s are done
- P2 or lower need user approval

## Markdown

- Projects are listed in priority order (P0 -> P4)
- Tasks should use `[ ]` syntax

## Org Mode

- Agents should leverage org mode's priorities, deadlines, schedules, and task management.
